    "result_text": "The jdbc profile's `UPDATE` already omits `namesake_count`/`household_id` (its insert uses `SimpleJdbcInsert` which auto-discovers columns), so `household_size` behaves exactly like the existing snapshot fields \u2014 no new inconsistency introduced. The default/tested profile is JPA, which maps everything.\n\nDone. All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\n`membershipTier` is a **read-time derived field** computed in `OwnerMapper.formatMembershipTier(owner)` purely from stored owner fields (like `namesakeCount`). GOLD depends on household size, which needs a value captured at creation and read back later, so I followed the existing `namesakeCount` snapshot pattern rather than adding DB access to the pure mapper.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `householdSize` field (`household_size` column) with getter/setter and `toString`, mirroring `namesakeCount`.\n\n2. **`controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 in `applyHouseholdPolicy` (which already computes the household members), record the post-create household size on the new owner: `owner.setHouseholdSize(members.size() + 1)`. A solo owner gets `1`; the third owner joining a shared household gets `3`.\n\n3. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 `formatMembershipTier` now returns `\"GOLD\"` first when `householdSize >= 3`, otherwise falls through to the existing SILVER/BRONZE rules unchanged.\n\n4. **Schema (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`)** \u2014 added the `household_size` column so the snapshot survives the read-back `GET`.\n\n5. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `membershipTier` description to document the GOLD rule.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreGoldForThreeMemberHousehold` (creates a 3-member shared household and asserts the third member is GOLD) passes, and the household-size computation lives in the household-policy method where the member list already exists \u2014 no logic was duplicated and no class became a catch-all.",
